WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Hello there! Perhaps you noticed that&nbsp;
Ms. Coffee Bean is fascinated by lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.640
and lots of data – too fascinated if you ask me.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:15.120
With the scaling law paper in Vision Transformers&nbsp;
and now with “How to train your ViT”,

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.800
we are seeing impressive performance of big&nbsp;
transformer models on lots and lots of data.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.080
With the release of “How to train your ViT?”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:30.880
more than 50,000 models – yes, 50 thousand! –&nbsp;
have been released by the Google Brain team.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.880
Which is great, because these models&nbsp;
do not have to be trained again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.440
and the whole community can start using them!

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.480
[alarm sound]

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.400
Ms. Coffee Bean, did you sound&nbsp;
the “foundation models” alarm?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.600
Oh, come on, who invited the term&nbsp;
“foundation models” into this video?

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.640
Ms. Coffee Bean, we need to talk…
about the question whether ViT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.680
counts as a foundation model, about&nbsp;
the term “foundation model” itself

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.320
and about some interesting points we&nbsp;
saw in the foundation model paper,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.440
like the question of "understanding"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.240
and generalization power of&nbsp;
models under distribution shift.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.000
Ms. Coffee Bean, are you ready for this?
Should we also sound the rant warning alarm?

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.960
Feel free to skip through the video&nbsp;
chapters when it gets too much.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.720
While reading the abstract of this new...
ah well, who are we kidding?

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.280
It’s Machine learning, so this is already old!

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.520
I was saying that I was reading the&nbsp;
abstract of “How to train your ViT?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.040
which explains to us how Vision Transformers&nbsp;
can be adapted to many downstream tasks.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.360
Oh! So this must be one of&nbsp;
these… foundation models, right?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.040
You know, this paper (rather a book)&nbsp;
published by everyone at Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.040
where they rename pre-trained&nbsp;
models into foundation models.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.520
Hmm… but there's no mention of ViT or DeiT,&nbsp;
just a reference among many others to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.600
paper about emerging properties&nbsp;
in vision transformers, that’s it.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.440
So why didn’t they mention ViT?
Honestly, no idea.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.280
Let’s check the definition of&nbsp;
foundation models to see for ourselves:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.000
“A foundation model is any model that&nbsp;
is trained on broad data at scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.600
and can be adapted (e.g., fine-tuned)&nbsp;
to a wide range of downstream tasks”.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.840
ViT is trained on more than 300M images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.520
but is this broad enough?
How broad is “broad data at scale”?

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.880
Is something like ImageNet with&nbsp;
1,2 Million images large scale?

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:45.200
I mean, it seems like 2009 it was, but nowadays&nbsp;
ImageNet counts as “academic sized dataset”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:51.360
and is put into contrast with the hundreds&nbsp;
of millions of images ViT was trained on.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.240
So is ImageNet now big or small?&nbsp;
What scale does the scale have?

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.880
Well, let’s suppose that the 300M images&nbsp;
for ViT, a scale that not many have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.480
is large scale enough. So the condition&nbsp;
of “broad data at scale” for ViT: check.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.120
Second condition: “can be adapted to a&nbsp;
wide range of downstream tasks”. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.600
how should we know from this definition&nbsp;
that this enumeration is wide enough?

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.440
What about now? How wide is wide?

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.080
Well… How is this definition of any help&nbsp;
in making terminology more accurate?

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.040
You see, me and Ms. Coffee Bean have a&nbsp;
love-hate relationship with the term.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:40.480
Ms. Coffee Bean, the YouTuber, loves it.
I am the PhD student and I am mad at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:46.000
as a small bystander to this PR-stunt&nbsp;
that seems to have worked quite well.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.200
Even if there are enough people&nbsp;
in AI who criticize this move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.480
there are people already happily start to&nbsp;
use this new term of “foundation models”.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.000
Small PhD student rant here,
but was it really a necessity that now every time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.880
one uses this term that fits everywhere and&nbsp;
just replaces some older, non-attributed terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.000
all these researchers have&nbsp;
to receive a whole citation?

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.680
Ok, but forget about this.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.680
Let’s look specifically at the section&nbsp;
where they explain why they chose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.200
name they did: They say that this term&nbsp;
“foundation models” will fill a void.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.160
Void I was not really aware of.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:33.200
We cite “Existing terms capture the technical&nbsp;
dimension”. Because the grandeur of a paradigm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.960
shift is not visible? So their idea&nbsp;
is: let’s replace exact technical terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.480
through a vague term wich is literary&nbsp;
and metaphoric in its interpretations,

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.080
because on one side, we have the confused&nbsp;
researcher about when to use or not to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.040
the term “foundation models” for&nbsp;
the ViT for example, or ResNet!

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.360
But on the other side, the uninformed broad public
-- and possibly supporters of this new “Center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.440
for Research on Foundation Models --,
should suddenly get it, that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.440
about models laying a foundation, but that&nbsp;
a foundation does not build an entire house?

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.200
This is deep.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.400
Oh wow, I am making Ms. Coffee Bean angrier&nbsp;
and angrier because she does not agree with me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.840
You know, Ms. Coffee Bean who was created&nbsp;
by a PR move herself, actually loves this!&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:31.360
It's nice, more drama, more PR, more catchy&nbsp;
terms for grant applications and paper titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.720
so do not forget to add this one to your list!

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.520
Even more, from the perspective of someone&nbsp;
who is not involved in research on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.400
models or from the perspective of someone who&nbsp;
likes to educate the broader public about AI,&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.960
what is really, really great about this&nbsp;
paper is that it is an extensive place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.400
to learn all about these big models for&nbsp;
anyone not closely familiar with them.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.480
It’s like a manual. So, she&nbsp;
wishes your happy reading of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.600
a document where researchers had no space limit!

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.280
Now, not really to spare you the reading,&nbsp;
but more to give you some spoilers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:13.600
here are Ms. Coffee Bean’s favorite takes from&nbsp;
this book... or paper of 200 pages; whatever:

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.960
It was really scary to read the section about&nbsp;
incentives where they say that, we cite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.560
“Just as the pharmaceutical industry has little&nbsp;
incentive to devote significant resources to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:32.000
research and development of malaria treatments,&nbsp;
because poor people cannot afford medications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.040
the tech industry has little incentive to&nbsp;
devote significant resources to technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.000
designed for improving the condition of poor&nbsp;
and marginalized people [Reich et al. 2021].”

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.600
So in contrast, universities&nbsp;
are much, much better?

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.280
But I don’t know if things are so&nbsp;
black-and-white. When Ms. Coffee Bean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:55.520
sees the broad-spread aversion of academics&nbsp;
for actually doing science communication

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.720
and that there are companies that&nbsp;
do better than described here,&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.800
we are not that sure.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.520
Then Ms. Coffee Bean and me both liked the&nbsp;
section on “Understanding” where it is all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.680
about the questions that are going on lately&nbsp;
after seeing how good these models become:&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.240
But do they “understand”? Does GPT-3 understand?

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:23.040
We even had a video previously about the&nbsp;
restricted question of whether a text-only system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.400
could ever understand asked by Bender and Koller

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.240
and a follow-up on what more is required to get&nbsp;
to understanding, based on a paper by Bisk et al.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.840
The view in the foundation&nbsp;
models paper is a little broader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.760
with snapshots taken from&nbsp;
different philosophical sides.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.840
And you know, when it gets philosophical, opinions&nbsp;
tend to diverge: The paper tells us that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:54.080
the metaphysical question of what understanding&nbsp;
really means. And then people’s answers diverge.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.440
But supposedly we get an answer there on which&nbsp;
we all agree, then we need to go further into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.280
the epistemological question -- which is a&nbsp;
fancy word for saying that we are unsure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:11.040
about whether we would ever be able to&nbsp;
exhaustively test and analyze or dissect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.960
the model to know whether it has understanding.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.640
This means that even if a&nbsp;
model would have understanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.440
we would not be able to say whether it does so,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.800
because we might not have the right means&nbsp;
or even the right measure for understanding.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.240
The authors highlight that it’s the same&nbsp;
with intelligence with the Turing test that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.120
has been passed many times already but we&nbsp;
still don’t call those systems intelligent&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.160
because we keep moving the goalpost.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.440
We even like to leave the question open of whether&nbsp;
understanding even matters in these models.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:51.280
Right now, we do not know, and people keep&nbsp;
disagreeing, exactly like me and Ms. Coffee Bean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:57.360
My opinion – me, as a researcher – is,&nbsp;
that understanding is a crucial component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.080
for these models. Only then we can trust&nbsp;
them, be comfortable with them and rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.360
on them when they do difficult things&nbsp;
like driving our cars or caring for us.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.360
And then there is Ms. Coffee Bean,&nbsp;
the incarnation of pragmatism:

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.080
She thinks that we do not&nbsp;
need models that understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.800
for us to comfortably interact with them.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.440
We interact with things that we do not&nbsp;
understand daily: When browsing a website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.160
most people do now know about
IP-addresses and DNS servers or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.040
any other technicalities of the Internet,&nbsp;
but they use and trust it, nonetheless.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.320
Ms. Coffee Bean thinks that in&nbsp;
the future, at the moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.200
these models will get close-to-perfect,&nbsp;
it will all become a risk-assessment:&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:45.200
Supposedly we know from lots of data statistics&nbsp;
that a self-driving car crashed 100 times in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.400
last 20 years and otherwise was driving&nbsp;
perfectly throughout the whole world.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.720
But we know that there were 1000 human drivers&nbsp;
that crashed due to alcohol consumption or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:03.840
whatever in the last 20 years. Do we really need&nbsp;
to know that that self-driving car understands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.640
to trust it more and to use it?

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.160
What do you think about this? Let us know.&nbsp;
The comment section is for you to crash.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:18.400
Otherwise, in the paper, it was nice to see&nbsp;
more emphasis put on the data creation process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.960
and the push to create a data hub for&nbsp;
the whole community, because these models&nbsp;

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:29.360
rarely (and by that we actually mean "never")&nbsp;
get any better than the data they are trained on.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:34.800
Then it is more surprising to see how the&nbsp;
point on distribution shift was set up.&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.160
So distribution shift is a point we&nbsp;
really thought we need to discuss.&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:45.280
It’s not about the message itself, with which we&nbsp;
agree, but about how the message is presented.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.800
We cite: “Existing work shows that&nbsp;
pretraining on unlabeled data is an effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:57.440
general-purpose way to improve accuracy on OOD&nbsp;
[which is out-of-domain] test distributions”.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.480
This, and almost every other Machine Learning&nbsp;
talk Ms. Coffee Bean heard, makes it sound like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.680
increasing the amount of training data&nbsp;
increases the out-of-domain performance.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.600
Don't get us wrong, we completely&nbsp;
agree that scaling up the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:18.800
increases performance. But we do not really&nbsp;
agree on this strong emphasis on OOD.&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:25.040
So OOD means that we have types of data that&nbsp;
the model has not seen. This of course in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:31.200
contrast with "in-distribution (ID)" which&nbsp;
is types of data the model has already seen.&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.960
I mean, look at the example here: in-distribution&nbsp;
is satellite data of the whole world since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:42.880
model was pretrained on that. Then how is&nbsp;
satellite data of Malawi out-of-distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:49.280
since Malawi is part of the world?&nbsp;
We are OOD sceptics in a sense.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.800
We have not seen these models&nbsp;
perform well out of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:59.840
comfort zone, which is OOD. What we have seen
is how expanding the amounts of training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:06.800
the in-domain distribution was expanded a lot too.
We just make the training data so huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.680
that most of the testing you do is&nbsp;
actually still in-training-distribution.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.200
In other words: nothing will ever be out&nbsp;
of training distribution if the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.520
world was seen during training.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:25.760
And combine this with our incapability&nbsp;
of really imagining huge data amounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:30.320
and we are ready to think that we have created&nbsp;
something more powerful than it actually is.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:38.080
Why can GPT-3 write HMTL code and perfect&nbsp;
this in few-shot learning? Because it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.440
HTML code during the pre-training stage already!

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:46.000
But why do we talk about data samples&nbsp;
like they were out-of-distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:52.000
when they are mostly an interpolated version&nbsp;
of a sparsely sampled (in)distribution?

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.200
The authors give the example of CLIP&nbsp;
performing well even on ImageNet Sketch.&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:02.960
But of course! It was trained on&nbsp;
400M images from the Internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.840
where enough of them were containing sketches!

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:11.600
To us it seems like “foundation models” are not&nbsp;
really a solution to the generalization problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.560
since one just completely resolves the&nbsp;
need for generalization by letting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.920
model interpolate or even specialize&nbsp;
during finetuning, but not generalize.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:26.720
We do not negate the advantages&nbsp;
of scaling up data; it works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:31.440
very well to increase the data so much&nbsp;
that everything becomes in-distribution.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.720
But we just advocate for more honesty&nbsp;
about where the performance gains of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.720
“foundation models” actually come from.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:44.880
If you are looking for real alternatives&nbsp;
and real out-of-domain generalization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.520
where your model can really handle new data that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:52.720
is substantially different from what it&nbsp;
has seen before, look in other places.&nbsp;

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:58.080
Someone in this community proposed&nbsp;
Jeff Hawkins’ HTM model. Check it out!

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:04.320
Otherwise, we really enjoyed and appreciated the&nbsp;
section on alignment where the authors say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.720
“One challenge is the misalignment&nbsp;
between the foundation model’s training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:13.600
objective and the desired behavior;”.
This really speaks from our heart and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.960
you want our take on this in relation to&nbsp;
CLIP, watch our video where we highlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:24.160
how alignment has already started to be&nbsp;
a problem, when CLIP’s normal behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:30.480
following its training objective was blamed to&nbsp;
have been the victim of an “adversarial attack”.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.880
Well, wow. This was the video.&nbsp;
Congrats for making it until the end.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.240
But what do you think about&nbsp;
"foundation models"? Let us know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:45.280
your perspective in the comments to help&nbsp;
settle my divergence with Ms. Coffee Bean!

00:14:45.280 --> 00:15:01.490
Do not forget to like and subscribe! Okay, bye!

